= A
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Analytic Number Theory}

= 1
{parent=2008/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/1}

A <multiplicative arithmetic function> has $f(1)=1$ and satisfies $f(mn)=f(m)f(n)$ whenever $m,n$ are coprime. For a <prime number>[prime] power $p^e$, the divisors are exactly $1,p,\ldots,p^e$, so there are $e+1$. Multiplicativity therefore gives
$$
\boxed{d(n)=\prod_{j=1}^k(e_j+1).}
$$
Fix $\varepsilon>0$. If $p\ge2^{1/\varepsilon}$, then $(e+1)p^{-\varepsilon e}\le(e+1)2^{-e}\le1$ for every <integer> $e\ge0$. There are only finitely many <prime numbers>[primes] below the threshold. For each such <prime number>[prime] the sequence $(e+1)p^{-\varepsilon e}$ tends to zero, so its supremum $C_p$ is finite and at least one. Thus
$$
\frac{d(n)}{n^\varepsilon}=\prod_{p^e\parallel n}(e+1)p^{-\varepsilon e}\le\prod_{p<2^{1/\varepsilon}}C_p=:C(\varepsilon).
$$
The product is over a fixed finite set, independent of $n$, proving $\boxed{d(n)\le C(\varepsilon)n^\varepsilon}$.

The change of divisor $k=m/d$ gives $\sigma(m)/m=\sum_{k\mid m}1/k$. List the divisors as $k_1<\cdots<k_{d(m)}$. They are distinct positive <integers>, so $k_j\ge j$ and $\sigma(m)/m\le\sum_{j\le d(m)}1/j$.

For an <integer> $K\ge1$, monotonicity of $1/x$ gives $\sum_{k=2}^K1/k\le\int_1^Kdx/x=\log K$; the same bound for real $K\ge1$ follows by taking its <integer> part. Consequently
$$
\frac{\sigma(m)}m\le1+\log d(m)\le\varepsilon\log m+1+\log C(\varepsilon).
$$
Hence the requested bound holds with $\boxed{K(\varepsilon)=1+\log C(\varepsilon)}$, including $m=1$.

= 2
{parent=2008/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/2}

For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, the <Euler product> theorem is
$$
\boxed{F(s)=\prod_{p}\left(\sum_{e=0}^\infty f(p^e)p^{-es}\right).}
$$
The local series converge absolutely, and the product is <absolutely convergent> in the sense that the sum over <prime numbers>[primes] of the absolute deviations of the local factors from one is finite. Indeed this sum is bounded by $\sum_{n\ge2}|f(n)|n^{-\operatorname{Re}s}$. To prove the identity, expand the product over <prime numbers>[primes] at most $y$. <Absolute convergence> permits all those local expansions, and multiplicativity identifies the <coefficients>[coefficients] with $f(n)$ for <integers> whose <prime number>[prime] factors are at most $y$. Every omitted <integer> exceeds $y$, so the difference from the full <Dirichlet series> is bounded by its absolute tail beyond $y$, which tends to zero. The same tail bound on a closed right half-plane inside $\operatorname{Re}s>1$ proves local <uniform convergence>. With signed <coefficients>[coefficients] a local factor can be zero; the identity does not require claiming every such general <Euler product> is nonzero.

For $d(n^2)$, multiplicativity gives local <coefficients>[coefficients] $d(p^{2e})=2e+1$. With $z=p^{-s}$,
$$
\sum_{e\ge0}(2e+1)z^e=\frac{1+z}{(1-z)^2}=\frac{1-z^2}{(1-z)^3}.
$$
The divisor bound from (1), applied with a sufficiently small exponent, proves <absolute convergence> when $\operatorname{Re}s>1$; divergence at one follows from $d(n^2)\ge1$. Multiplying the local identities therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{d(n^2)}{n^s}=\frac{\zeta(s)^3}{\zeta(2s)}.}
$$
For $n\le x$, the inequality $n^\delta\le x^\delta$ gives $d(n^2)/n\le x^\delta d(n^2)/n^{1+\delta}$. Sum those nonnegative terms and enlarge to the whole infinite series to get the required comparison.

For real $1<s<2$, integral comparison gives $\zeta(s)\le1+(s-1)^{-1}\le2/(s-1)$, while $\zeta(2s)\ge1$. Hence $\zeta(s)^3/\zeta(2s)=O((s-1)^{-3})$ uniformly in that interval. Choosing $\delta=1/\log x$ for $x\ge3$ makes $0<\delta<1$ and $x^\delta=e$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\le x}\frac{d(n^2)}n=O((\log x)^3).}
$$

= 3
{parent=2008/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/3}

Each finite-interval integral $f_n(s)$ is entire in $s$: the integrand is $ (x-n)e^{-(s+1)\log x}$ and its complex derivatives may be integrated on the compact interval. On a compact subset of $\operatorname{Re}s>0$, choose $\delta>0$ with $\operatorname{Re}s\ge\delta$. Then $|f_n(s)|\le n^{-1-\delta}$. The Weierstrass uniform-convergence theorem gives convergence and holomorphy of $\sum_nf_n(s)$ throughout that half-plane.

<Integration by parts> gives
$$
sf_n(s)=-(n+1)^{-s}+\int_n^{n+1}x^{-s}\,dx.
$$
For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, sum absolutely and use the integral over $[1,\infty)$ to obtain $s\sum_nf_n(s)=1-\zeta(s)+1/(s-1)$. Rearranging gives the first requested identity.

On $[n,n+1]$, the magnitude of $(x-n)(x-n-1)$ is at most $1/4$. On a compact subset of $\operatorname{Re}s>-1$, its integrals are bounded by a convergent constant multiple of $n^{-1-\delta}$ for some $\delta>0$. Thus $\sum_ng_n(s)$ is <holomorphic> there. The prefactor $s(s+1)$ is entire, leaving only the explicit <simple pole> at one in $\widetilde\zeta$, with <residue> one.

Differentiate $(x-n)(x-n-1)x^{-s-1}$ and integrate. Its endpoint values both vanish, giving
$$
\boxed{(s+1)g_n(s)-2f_n(s)=-\int_n^{n+1}x^{-s-1}\,dx=\frac{(n+1)^{-s}-n^{-s}}s.}
$$
At $s=0$ the last quotient has its continuous value $-\log((n+1)/n)$. For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, telescoping over $n$ gives $(s+1)\sum_ng_n-2\sum_nf_n=-1/s$. Substitution into $\widetilde\zeta$ yields
$$
\widetilde\zeta(s)=\frac1{s-1}+1-s\sum_nf_n(s)=\frac s{s-1}-s\sum_nf_n(s)=\zeta(s).
$$
This supplies the <analytic continuation> to $\operatorname{Re}s>-1$ apart from the <pole>. Evaluating its <holomorphic> expression at zero makes the series term vanish and gives $\boxed{\zeta(0)=-1/2}$.

= 4
{parent=2008/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/4}

For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, logarithmic differentiation of the <absolutely convergent> <Euler product> gives $-\zeta'(s)/\zeta(s)=\sum_{n\ge1}\Lambda(n)n^{-s}$, where $\Lambda(p^k)=\log p$ and it is zero otherwise. On the line $\operatorname{Re}s=c>1$, this series is absolutely bounded by $\sum_n(\log n)n^{-c}<\infty$, and $1/[s(s+1)]$ is integrable in the vertical variable. Hence <absolute convergence> justifies interchanging sum and <contour integral>. The given first Mellin kernel, with $y=x/n$, contributes $x(1-n/x)=x-n$ if $n\le x$ and zero otherwise. Thus
$$
\boxed{\psi_1(x)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{c-i\infty}^{c+i\infty}-\frac{\zeta'(s)}{\zeta(s)}\frac{x^{s+1}}{s(s+1)}\,ds.}
$$
Splitting off $1/(s-1)$ gives the desired decomposition with $f$ for $x\ge1$. There is a range misprint in the second supplied kernel: for $0<y<1$ its integral is zero, not $(y-1)^2/2$. The globally correct value is $(y-1)_+^2/2$. For $y\ge1$ it is exactly the expression used here, so the subsequent large-$x$ argument is unchanged.

Near one, write $h=s-1$ and $\zeta(s)=h^{-1}+\gamma+O(h)$. Then $-\zeta'/\zeta=h^{-1}-\gamma+O(h)$, so subtraction cancels the <pole> and $f$ has the removable value $\boxed{f(1)=-\gamma/2}$.

For a continuously differentiable $F$ with the given decay, both $F$ and $F'$ are integrable and $F(t)\to0$ at either end. <Integration by parts> on finite intervals and passage to the limit gives, for $x>1$,
$$
\int_{-\infty}^\infty F(t)x^{it}\,dt=-\frac1{i\log x}\int_{-\infty}^\infty F'(t)x^{it}\,dt.
$$
Its absolute value is at most $\|F'\|_1/\log x$, proving the estimate.

We also need uniform control of $f(c+it)$ on bounded $t$ as $c\downarrow1$. Here is the <three-four-one zero-free-region argument> supplying it. For $\sigma>1$, the <Euler product> and $3+4\cos u+\cos2u=2(1+\cos u)^2\ge0$ imply
$$
\zeta(\sigma)^3|\zeta(\sigma+it)|^4|\zeta(\sigma+2it)|\ge1.
$$
If $\zeta(1+it)$ vanished for a nonzero real $t$, holomorphy from (3) would make the left side $O((\sigma-1)^{-3+4})\to0$, since $\zeta(\sigma+2it)$ remains bounded, a contradiction. Thus there are no zeros on that line, and there are none to its right by the <Euler product>. After removal of the singularity at one, $f$ and $f'$ are bounded on the compact rectangle $1\le\operatorname{Re}s\le2$, $|\operatorname{Im}s|\le3$.

For $|t|\ge3$, put $H=-\zeta'/\zeta-1/(s-1)$. The supplied estimates give $H=O((\log|t|)^9)$ and
$$
H'=-\frac{\zeta''}{\zeta}+\left(\frac{\zeta'}{\zeta}\right)^2+\frac1{(s-1)^2}=O((\log|t|)^{18}),
$$
uniformly in $1<c<2$. Since $f=H/[s(s+1)]$, it follows that $f=O(t^{-2}(\log|t|)^9)$ and $f'=O(t^{-2}(\log|t|)^{18})$, both $O(|t|^{-3/2})$. Also $d f(c+it)/dt=if'(c+it)$. Together with the compact bound this proves both required decay bounds with constants independent of $c$.

Parametrizing the contour, the remainder in the decomposition is $x^{c+1}/(2\pi)$ times the <oscillatory integral> just bounded. Choose $c=1+1/\log x$ for $x>e$, so $x^{c+1}=ex^2$. Hence the remainder is $O(x^2/\log x)$. Finally $(x-1)^2/2=x^2/2+O(x)$, and $O(x)$ is absorbed, proving
$$
\boxed{\psi_1(x)=\frac{x^2}2+O\left(\frac{x^2}{\log x}\right).}
$$

= B
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Elliptic Curves}

= 5
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/i}

For $p=3$, a <root of a polynomial>[root] would have <valuation> zero because five is a <unit in a ring>[unit]. It would therefore reduce to a <unit in a ring>[unit] cube modulo nine. The only cube <residue classes> modulo nine are $0,1,-1$, none equal to five. Thus **no <root of a polynomial>[root] exists in $\mathbb Q_3$**.

For $p=5$, the <valuation> equation would be $3v_5(x)=1$, impossible because the <valuation> of a nonzero element of $\mathbb Q_5$ is an <integer>. Thus **no <root of a polynomial>[root] exists in $\mathbb Q_5$**.

For $p=13$, $7^3\equiv5\pmod{13}$ and the <derivative> $3\cdot7^2\equiv4\not\equiv0\pmod{13}$. <Hensel's lemma> lifts this simple <root of a polynomial>[root] to a <root of a polynomial>[root] in $\mathbb Z_{13}\subseteq\mathbb Q_{13}$. Thus **a <root of a polynomial>[root] exists in $\mathbb Q_{13}$**.

= ii
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/ii}

The expansion is
$$
\boxed{-\frac{13}8=1+3+2\cdot3^2+3^3+2\cdot3^4+3^5+2\cdot3^6+\cdots=1,\overline{12}\quad\text{in base }3.}
$$
The notation has the units digit before the comma and successive positive powers afterwards, as defined in the source. To check it, the <geometric series> converges in the <p-adic absolute value>[3-adic norm] and its sum is $1+(3+2\cdot3^2)/(1-3^2)=1-21/8=-13/8$.

For the general repeating block, $|p^2|_p<1$ gives
$$
\boxed{a_0,\overline{a_1a_2}=a_0+\frac{a_1p+a_2p^2}{1-p^2}=\frac{a_0(1-p^2)+a_1p+a_2p^2}{1-p^2}.}
$$
Both numerator and denominator are <integers>, and the denominator is a $p$-adic <unit in a ring>[unit].

= iii
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/iii}

The reduction has equation $F=x^3+y^3=0$. At the origin both first <partial derivatives> vanish, so the point is singular, also when $p=3$. A lift reducing to the origin would require $x,y\in p\mathbb Z_p$. Its two cubes would then lie in $p^3\mathbb Z_p$, whereas the right side of the original equation has <valuation> one. This is impossible.

For an example which does lift, choose
$$
\boxed{\mathcal D:x^3+y^3=p^3.}
$$
It has the same reduction and the <rational point> $(p,0)$ reduces to the origin. Over $\mathbb Q_p$, which has <characteristic> zero, a singular affine point would require $x=y=0$, which does not lie on this curve. Its <projective closure> is also smooth: the simultaneous <partial derivatives> of $X^3+Y^3-p^3Z^3$ cannot vanish at a projective point. Thus this is a nonsingular curve for every <prime number>[prime] $p$, including three. A <singular point> in the reduction can therefore fail to lift or can lift, depending on the original equation.

= iv
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/iv}

Let $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ be the nonzero reductions of $a,b,c$. The square set in $\mathbb F_p$ has $(p+1)/2$ members, including zero. Multiplication by $\alpha$, and separately the transformation $z\mapsto\gamma-\beta z$, preserve this cardinality. Hence the two sets in the hint have total cardinality $p+1>p$ and must intersect. This gives a solution $(x_0,y_0)$ modulo $p$.

The solution cannot have both coordinates zero since $\gamma\ne0$. If $x_0\ne0$, choose any integral lift of $y_0$ and apply <Hensel's lemma> to the <polynomial> $aX^2+by_0^2-c$ in $X$: its <derivative> at the reduced <root of a polynomial>[root] is $2\alpha x_0\ne0$. If $y_0\ne0$, use the other variable instead. The nonzero <derivative> uses $p\ne2$. Thus a solution lifts to $\boxed{x,y\in\mathbb Z_p}$ as required.

= 6
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/i}

For a nonsingular curve $y^2=x^3+Ax+B$ with <integers> $A,B$, the <Nagell–Lutz theorem> states that a finite <torsion subgroup>[rational torsion] point has <integer> coordinates and satisfies either $y=0$ or
$$
\boxed{y^2\mid4A^3+27B^2.}
$$
The point at infinity is the identity and is treated separately. Integrality is the result allowed in the source. To prove the remaining divisibility, suppose $y\ne0$. Then the doubled point is finite and also torsion, so its coordinates are integral. Its <tangent> slope is $m=(3x^2+A)/(2y)$ and its abscissa is $m^2-2x$. Thus $m^2$ is an <integer>. A <rational number> whose square is an <integer> is itself an <integer>, so $2y$ divides $3x^2+A$. In particular $y^2$ divides $\psi_1(x)=(3x^2+A)^2$. It also divides $\psi_2(x)=x^3+Ax+B=y^2$. Evaluating the permitted <polynomial> identity at the <integer> $x$ therefore proves the divisibility of $4A^3+27B^2$ by $y^2$.

For $y=0$, the point equals its negative and has order two. The theorem gives a finite set of torsion candidates, but the divisibility condition by itself does not assert that every candidate is torsion.

= ii
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/ii}

Here $4A^3+27B^2=31$. A nonzero ordinate of a <torsion point of an elliptic curve>[torsion point] must have its square dividing 31, so $y=\pm1$. The curve equation then becomes $x(x^2+1)=0$, whose only <integer> solution is $x=0$. No point with $y=0$ exists over the rationals: the monic cubic $x^3+x+1$ has no <integer> <root of a polynomial>[root], since its only possible <integer> <roots of a polynomial>[roots] are $\pm1$ and neither works.

It remains to test $(0,\pm1)$. Doubling either gives abscissa $(1/(\pm2))^2=1/4$, which is not an <integer>. If the original point were torsion its double would satisfy the integrality part of the <Nagell–Lutz theorem>, a contradiction. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}=\{\mathbf o\}.}
$$
The <rational point> $(0,1)$ consequently has infinite order, so its multiples give infinitely many distinct <rational points>.

= iii
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/iii}

The curve has <good reduction> at five and seven: its <discriminant> is $-432D^2$, and the hypotheses make $D$ a <unit in a ring>[unit] at those <prime numbers>[primes]. Over $\mathbb F_5$ the cubing map is a bijection, so
$$
\#E(\mathbb F_5)=1+\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_5}(1+\chi(x^3+D))=6.
$$
The character sum vanishes after replacing $x^3+D$ by a variable ranging over the whole <field>. Over $\mathbb F_7$, where $D=2$, the possible cubes are zero once, one three times, and minus one three times. The corresponding right sides are two, three and one. Two and one are nonzero squares while three is not, so the count is $1+2+0+6=9$.

<torsion subgroup>[Rational torsion] injects into the reduction group at a good odd <prime number>[prime], a standard reduction theorem. Thus its order divides $\gcd(6,9)=3$. If a point of order three existed, its coordinates would be integral by the <Nagell–Lutz theorem>. Since $2P=-P$, the <elliptic curve group law>[duplication formula] gives
$$
\frac{9x^4}{4y^2}-2x=x,\qquad3x(x^3+4D)=0.
$$
Thus either $x=0$ and $y^2=D$, or $x^3=-4D$ and $y^2=-3D$.

At the <prime number>[prime] in the hypothesis, $D$ is a nonsquare <unit in a ring>[unit]. Since $p\equiv1\pmod3$, there is a nontrivial cube <root of unity> $\zeta\in\mathbb F_p$, and $(2\zeta+1)^2=-3$. Hence $-3$ is a nonzero square, so both $D$ and $-3D$ are nonsquares. Neither ordinate equation is possible even modulo $p$. There is no <rational point> of order three, proving
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}=\{\mathbf o\}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/iv}

Write the equation as $y^2=x^3-5x^2+4x$. Its <elliptic curve group law>[duplication formula] uses slope $m=(3x^2-10x+4)/(2y)$ and $x(2P)=m^2+5-2x$. At the three listed points the slopes are respectively $i$, $-1+i\sqrt3$, and $2+\sqrt3$. Substitution gives
$$
\boxed{2P_1=(0,0),\qquad2P_2=(1,0),\qquad2P_3=(4,0).}
$$
These are nonidentity points of order two, so each listed point has exactly order four.

For $p\ne2,3$ the three distinct <roots of a polynomial>[roots] zero, one and four give the full two-<torsion subgroup> of order four over $\mathbb F_p$. At least one of $-1,3,-3$ is a square: if neither of the first two is, their product is. If $-1$ is a square, the first listed point exists over the <field>. If three is a square, the third exists. If $-3$ is a square, the second can be written $(1-\sqrt{-3},3+\sqrt{-3})$ and exists without requiring the two separate square <roots of a polynomial>[roots]. The duplication computations remain valid after reduction; their ordinates do not vanish unless $p=2$ or three. Thus a rational <2-torsion>[two-torsion] point has a half over the <field>. Together with a different <2-torsion>[two-torsion] point this generates a <subgroup> of order eight. Consequently $\boxed{8\mid\#E(\mathbb F_p)}$ for every stated <prime number>[prime].

Over the rationals there are already four <2-torsion>[two-torsion] points, including the identity. Counting at five gives eight points: the abscissas zero, one and four give one ordinate each, two and three give two each, and there is the identity. Thus <torsion subgroup>[rational torsion] has order dividing eight.

There are no <rational points> of order four. For a curve $y^2=\prod_j(x-e_j)$, translating $e_i$ to zero and using the <elliptic curve group law>[duplication formula] shows that a half of $(e_i,0)$ must satisfy $(x-e_i)^2=(e_i-e_j)(e_i-e_k)$. For the three <roots of a polynomial>[roots] here, the candidate abscissas are $\pm2$, $1\pm\sqrt{-3}$, and $4\pm2\sqrt3$. The last two pairs are nonrational; at $x=2,-2$ the ordinate squares are $-4,-36$, also impossible over $\mathbb Q$. A group of order eight containing this two-<torsion subgroup> would contain an element of order four, because an <elliptic curve> has only four points killed by two. Hence
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}\cong C_2\times C_2,\qquad\#E(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}=4.}
$$

= 7
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/b/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/7/i}

For a point with $u\ne0$, consider the <valuation> $v_\ell$ at any rational <prime number>[prime]. If $v_\ell(u)<0$, the term $u^3$ has strictly lower <valuation> than $a_1u^2$ and $b_1u$, since the <coefficients>[coefficients] are integral. Thus $2v_\ell(v)=3v_\ell(u)$, forcing $v_\ell(u)$ even. If $v_\ell(u)>0$ and $\ell\nmid b_1$, the factor $u^2+a_1u+b_1$ is a <unit in a ring>[unit], so $2v_\ell(v)=v_\ell(u)$, again even. At <valuation> zero there is no odd exponent to remove.

Therefore every odd <valuation> in the <square class> of $u$ lies at a <prime number>[prime] dividing $b_1$, and all negative valuations are even. Removing rational squares gives a signed <squarefree integer> $r$ with $r\mid b_1$. For $(0,0)$ the assigned class is that of $b_1$, whose <squarefree integer>[squarefree] representative also divides $b_1$; the identity has representative one. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{im}q\subseteq\{[r]:r\text{ is a signed squarefree divisor of }b_1\}.}
$$
The brackets stress that these <integers> represent <square classes>, which is the target of $q$.

= ii
{parent=2008/b/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/7/ii}

For <rank-one two-isogeny descent with parameters three and minus three>, use the standard <two-isogeny rank formula>. For $C:y^2=x(x^2+ax+b)$ and its dual curve $D:y^2=x(x^2-2ax+a^2-4b)$, let $\alpha_C,\alpha_D$ be the abscissa <square class>[square-class] maps, with their usual values at $(0,0)$ and the identity. If $r_C$ is the <rank of an elliptic curve>[Mordell–Weil rank], then
$$
\boxed{2^{r_C+2}=|\operatorname{im}\alpha_C|\,|\operatorname{im}\alpha_D|.}
$$
For the present curve $a=3,b=-3$, its possible classes are $1,-1,3,-3$, and the identity and $(0,0)$ supply $1,-3$. Exclude the class three as follows. A point in that class has $x=3(U/V)^2$, with coprime <integers> $U,V$, and substitution in the curve gives the necessary quartic equation
$$
N^2=3U^4+3U^2V^2-V^4
$$
with <integer> $N$. Modulo three this forces $3\mid V,N$. Coprimality then makes $U$ a <unit in a ring>[unit] modulo three, and modulo nine the right side is $3U^4\equiv3$, while $N^2\equiv0$, a contradiction. The class minus one is excluded as well, since multiplying it by the known class minus three would give three. Therefore the first image has size two.

The dual curve is $D:y^2=x(x^2-6x+21)$. Its quadratic factor is $(x-3)^2+12>0$ on the <real line>, so every nonzero rational abscissa is positive. By (i) its image is contained in $\{1,3,7,21\}$. The point $(0,0)$ supplies 21 and the point $(3,6)$ supplies three. As the <square class>[square-class] map is a homomorphism, their product supplies seven. All four occur. Hence the descent formula gives $2^{r_C+2}=2\cdot4=8$, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}C(\mathbb Q)=1.}
$$

= 8
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/b/8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/b/8/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/8/a/i}

Fix $P$ and put $e_m=h_x(mP)-m^2h_x(P)$ for $m\ge0$. Then $e_0=e_1=0$. Apply the given approximate parallelogram identity with its two arguments $mP$ and $P$. The quadratic terms cancel exactly, leaving
$$
|e_{m+1}-2e_m+e_{m-1}|\le C\qquad(m\ge1).
$$
Set $d_m=e_m-e_{m-1}$. Starting from $d_1=0$, the inequality gives $|d_m|\le(m-1)C$. Summing from one to $m$ therefore yields
$$
\boxed{|h_x(mP)-m^2h_x(P)|\le\frac{m(m-1)}2C.}
$$
Thus $C_m=m(m-1)C/2$ works independently of $P$, including $m=1$.

= ii
{parent=2008/b/8/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/8/a/ii}

The preceding bound at $m=2$ gives $|h_x(2Q)-4h_x(Q)|\le C$ for every point $Q$. For $a_n=4^{-n}h_x(2^nP)$, take $Q=2^nP$ to obtain
$$
|a_{n+1}-a_n|\le C4^{-n-1}.
$$
For $m>n$, summing the geometric tail gives
$$
\boxed{|a_m-a_n|\le\sum_{j=n}^\infty C4^{-j-1}=\frac C{3\cdot4^n}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
Thus the sequence is <Cauchy sequence>[Cauchy] and converges by <completeness of the real numbers>. The estimate also gives a quantitative bound on its distance from the limit.

